<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SLIDE 3 — MRI IN DEMENTIA
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="slide slide-content" data-toc="MRI Findings">
  <h2>Structural MRI — Hallmark Findings</h2>
  <div class="cards cards-2">
    <div class="card card-img">
      <h3>Alzheimer's Disease <span class="badge badge-ad">AD</span></h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Hippocampal &amp; entorhinal atrophy</strong> — earliest MRI sign; Scheltens MTA scale ≥2 (age-adjusted) is a key visual rating</li>
        <li>Posterior cortical thinning (parietal, precuneus) on FreeSurfer volumetry</li>
        <li>White matter changes (mild-moderate) — not a primary feature</li>
        <li>Lobar microbleeds if CAA co-exists; important for anti-amyloid therapy selection</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Jack et al., Alzheimers Dement 2024; Scheltens et al., Lancet 2021</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-img">
      <h3>Vascular Dementia <span class="badge badge-vasc">VaD</span></h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>White matter hyperintensities (WMH)</strong> — Fazekas scale ≥2; periventricular &amp; subcortical</li>
        <li>Lacunar infarcts in strategic areas (thalamus, basal ganglia, internal capsule)</li>
        <li>Cortical/territorial infarcts for multi-infarct dementia</li>
        <li>CADASIL: bilateral anterior temporal pole WMH (O'Sullivan sign)</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Román et al., Neurology 1993 (NINDS-AIREN); Wardlaw et al., Lancet Neurol 2013</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-img">
      <h3>Frontotemporal Dementia <span class="badge badge-ftd">FTD</span></h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Frontal &amp; anterior temporal lobe atrophy</strong> — "knife-blade" gyri on axial MRI</li>
        <li>bvFTD: bilateral frontal &gt; temporal (orbitofrontal, anterior cingulate)</li>
        <li>PNFA: left perisylvian; SD: left &gt; right temporal pole atrophy</li>
        <li>Asymmetric pattern helps distinguish from AD</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Rascovsky et al., Brain 2011; Seeley et al., Ann Neurol 2009</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-img">
      <h3>DLB &amp; PDD <span class="badge badge-dlb">DLB</span> <span class="badge badge-pdd">PDD</span></h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Relative preservation of hippocampal/medial temporal volume</strong> — distinguishes from AD</li>
        <li>Occipital cortex hypometabolism/atrophy</li>
        <li>Substantia nigra neuromelanin-sensitive MRI: reduced signal</li>
        <li>Locus coeruleus atrophy detectable on neuromelanin MRI</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">McKeith et al., Neurology 2017 (4th DLB Consortium); Grinberg et al., J Neuropathol 2009</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SLIDE 4 — CT IN DEMENTIA
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="slide slide-content" data-toc="CT Findings">
  <h2>CT Brain — Role &amp; Key Findings</h2>
  <div class="info-box">
    <p>CT remains the first-line neuroimaging in most acute settings and is mandated by many dementia guidelines (NICE, AAN) to exclude reversible causes before MRI. It lacks the soft-tissue contrast of MRI but is widely available and rapid.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cards cards-3" style="margin-top:16px;">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Primary Uses</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>Exclude structural causes (SDH, NPH, tumour)</li>
        <li>Detect calcifications (FAHR disease, hypoparathyroidism)</li>
        <li>Visualise global/focal atrophy</li>
        <li>Exclude large cortical/subcortical infarcts</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>AD on CT</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>Medial temporal lobe &amp; sylvian fissure widening</li>
        <li>Generalised sulcal enlargement relative to age</li>
        <li>Third ventricle dilation</li>
        <li>Sensitivity lower than MRI for early atrophy</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Frisoni et al., Nat Rev Neurol 2010</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>NPH &amp; Other</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus</strong>: Evans index &gt;0.3; periventricular lucency; tight high-convexity sulci (disproportionately enlarged subarachnoid space hydrocephalus, DESH)</li>
        <li>Chronic SDH: crescent hypodense collection</li>
        <li>Fazekas WMH grading also applicable on CT</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Relkin et al., Neurosurgery 2005; Jaraj et al., Neurology 2014</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:16px;">
    <p><strong>Key Point:</strong> When CT is normal and clinical suspicion remains high, MRI (volumetric 3D T1) is the preferred next step. CT–MRI correlation is essential in vascular dementia where WMH subtypes differ in prognosis.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SLIDE 5 — AMYLOID PET
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="slide slide-content" data-toc="Amyloid PET">
  <h2>Amyloid PET — The "A" Biomarker</h2>
  <div class="cards cards-2">
    <div class="card card-img">
      <h3>Tracers &amp; Regulatory Status</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Florbetapir (¹⁸F-AV45)</strong> — FDA approved 2012</li>
        <li><strong>Flutemetamol (¹⁸F)</strong> — FDA/EMA approved 2013</li>
        <li><strong>Florbetaben (¹⁸F-NaF)</strong> — FDA approved 2014</li>
        <li>All show cortical-to-cerebellum SUVR elevation with amyloid positivity</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Clark et al., JAMA 2011; Barthel et al., Lancet Neurol 2011</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-img">
      <h3>Interpretation</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Visual read</strong>: grey-white matter contrast loss in neocortex → positive</li>
        <li><strong>SUVR quantification</strong>: centiloid scale (0–100 range) standardises across tracers</li>
        <li>Precuneus, posterior cingulate, lateral temporal, frontal cortex affected earliest</li>
        <li>Centiloid &gt;24.4 = amyloid positive (Klunk et al., <em>Alzheimers Dement</em> 2015)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-img">
      <h3>Clinical Utility</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>Approved appropriate-use criteria: MCI or dementia with diagnostic uncertainty (AUC, Johnson et al., <em>J Nucl Med</em> 2013)</li>
        <li>Negative amyloid PET essentially excludes AD (NPV &gt;94%)</li>
        <li>Now required for lecanemab/donanemab trial enrolment and clinical use</li>
        <li>CMS 2024: Coverage with Evidence Development — IDEAS registry data</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Rabinovici et al., JAMA 2019 (IDEAS study)</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-img">
      <h3>Amyloid in Non-AD Dementias</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>DLB: ~50% amyloid-positive (mixed pathology)</li>
        <li>FTD: typically amyloid-negative; positive result argues against FTD</li>
        <li>VaD: amyloid can be incidental (age-related); does not confirm AD</li>
        <li>CAA: diffuse cortical amyloid accumulation</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SLIDE 6 — TAU PET
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="slide slide-content" data-toc="Tau PET">
  <h2>Tau PET — The "T" Biomarker</h2>
  <div class="info-box">
    <p>Tau PET measures neurofibrillary tangle (NFT) burden in vivo. Unlike amyloid PET, tau patterns track disease <strong>stage</strong> and <strong>clinical phenotype</strong>, making it a powerful prognostic tool.</p>
    <span class="cite">Ossenkoppele et al., Science 2016; Leuzy et al., Nat Rev Neurol 2023</span>
  </div>
  <div class="cards cards-3" style="margin-top:14px;">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>First-Generation: ¹⁸F-Flortaucipir (AV-1451)</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>FDA approved May 2020 for AD evaluation</li>
        <li>Mesial temporal (EC/hippocampus) → isocortex spread mirrors Braak staging</li>
        <li>Braak I–II: entorhinal; III–IV: limbic; V–VI: neocortical</li>
        <li>Off-target binding: choroid plexus, meninges, melanocytes</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Second-Generation Tracers</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>¹⁸F-MK-6240</strong>: lower off-target; sensitive early-stage</li>
        <li><strong>¹⁸F-PI-2620</strong>: binds 3R and 4R tau (useful in PSP, CBD)</li>
        <li><strong>¹⁸F-RO-948</strong>: high specificity for AD tau</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Leuzy et al., Nat Rev Neurol 2023</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Tau Spread Patterns by Syndrome</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Typical AD</strong>: posterior-predominant neocortical tau</li>
        <li><strong>PCA</strong>: occipital–parietal tau</li>
        <li><strong>lvPPA</strong>: left perisylvian tau</li>
        <li><strong>PSP</strong>: subthalamic nucleus, globus pallidus, midbrain (PI-2620)</li>
        <li><strong>CBD</strong>: asymmetric cortical + subcortical</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:14px;">
    <strong>2024 Update:</strong> NIA-AA 2024 Revised Criteria include tau PET as a core biomarker alongside amyloid and neurodegeneration markers. Tau PET is now used clinically to stage AD for anti-tau therapeutic trials (e.g., semorinemab, zagotenemab).
    <span class="cite">Jack et al., Alzheimers Dement 2024</span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SLIDE 7 — FDG-PET & SPECT
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="slide slide-content" data-toc="FDG-PET &amp; SPECT">
  <h2>FDG-PET &amp; SPECT — Functional Imaging</h2>
  <div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Modality / Tracer</th>
          <th>Dementia Syndrome</th>
          <th>Characteristic Pattern</th>
          <th>Key Reference</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>¹⁸F-FDG PET</strong></td>
          <td><span class="badge badge-ad">AD</span></td>
          <td>Bilateral temporoparietal &amp; posterior cingulate hypometabolism; frontal in late stage</td>
          <td class="cell-m">Minoshima et al., J Nucl Med 1997</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>¹⁸F-FDG PET</td>
          <td><span class="badge badge-ftd">bvFTD</span></td>
          <td>Bilateral anterior frontal &amp; anterior temporal hypometabolism; relative parietal sparing</td>
          <td class="cell-m">Diehl-Schmid et al., Eur J Nucl Med 2007</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>¹⁸F-FDG PET</td>
          <td><span class="badge badge-dlb">DLB</span></td>
          <td>Posterior cortical + <strong>occipital</strong> hypometabolism; "cingulate island sign" (preserved posterior cingulate)</td>
          <td class="cell-m">McKeith et al., Neurology 2017</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>¹⁸F-FDG PET</td>
          <td>CBD / PSP</td>
          <td>CBD: asymmetric cortical-subcortical; PSP: midbrain &amp; frontal hypometabolism</td>
          <td class="cell-m">Whitwell et al., Neurology 2017</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>¹²³I-FP-CIT SPECT</strong> (DaTSCAN)</td>
          <td><span class="badge badge-dlb">DLB</span> / <span class="badge badge-pdd">PDD</span></td>
          <td>Reduced striatal dopamine transporter uptake (putamen &gt; caudate); comma → dot sign</td>
          <td class="cell-m">McKeith et al., Neurology 2017 (Indicative biomarker)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>¹²³I-MIBG SPECT</td>
          <td><span class="badge badge-dlb">DLB</span></td>
          <td>Reduced cardiac sympathetic innervation (heart-to-mediastinum ratio &lt;1.6); separates DLB from PD/PSNP</td>
          <td class="cell-m">Yoshita et al., Neurology 2006</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Tc-99m HMPAO/ECD SPECT</td>
          <td><span class="badge badge-ad">AD</span></td>
          <td>Temporoparietal perfusion deficit; widely available; lower resolution than FDG-PET</td>
          <td class="cell-m">NICE guideline NG97 2018</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Tc-99m HMPAO SPECT</td>
          <td><span class="badge badge-ftd">FTD</span></td>
          <td>Frontal hypoperfusion; may help differentiate from AD when MRI equivocal</td>
          <td class="cell-m">Diehl-Schmid et al., Eur J Nucl Med 2007</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="info-box" style="margin-top:14px;">
    <strong>DaTSCAN specificity &gt;85%</strong> for separating DLB/PDD from AD/VaD. Now included as an <em>indicative biomarker</em> in the 4th DLB Consortium criteria (McKeith et al., <em>Neurology</em> 2017).
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SLIDE 8 — EEG IN DEMENTIA
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="slide slide-content" data-toc="EEG Findings">
  <h2>Electroencephalography (EEG)</h2>
  <div class="cards cards-2">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>AD — EEG Signatures</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Diffuse slowing</strong>: progressive increase in theta/delta; alpha peak frequency slows (normal 8–12 Hz → &lt;8 Hz in moderate AD)</li>
        <li><strong>Decreased alpha/beta coherence</strong> between frontal-parietal regions</li>
        <li><strong>Increased delta power</strong> correlates with cognitive decline (MMSE correlation r ≈ −0.60)</li>
        <li>Quantitative EEG (qEEG): spectral analysis shows ratio of slow-to-fast bands ↑</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Jeong et al., Clin Neurophysiol 2004; Babiloni et al., Clin Neurophysiol 2016</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>DLB — Distinctive EEG</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Prominent slow-wave activity (&lt;8 Hz)</strong> more severe than AD at equivalent MMSE</li>
        <li><strong>Fluctuating dominant frequency</strong> — correlates with fluctuating cognition (a core DLB feature)</li>
        <li><strong>Temporal intermittent rhythmic delta activity (TIRDA)</strong> — reported in DLB</li>
        <li>Pre-alpha transients in occipital regions</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Bonanni et al., J Neurol Neurosurg Psychiatry 2008; McKeith et al., Neurology 2017</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>CJD — Pathognomonic EEG</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Periodic sharp wave complexes (PSWCs)</strong> — triphasic morphology, 1–2 Hz, generalized</li>
        <li>Present in ~65% sporadic CJD; ~90% in late stage</li>
        <li>Part of WHO diagnostic criteria for sCJD</li>
        <li>EEG sensitivity ~66%, specificity ~74% for PSWCs in sCJD</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">WHO diagnostic criteria for CJD; Zerr et al., Brain 2009</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>FTD &amp; Other</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>FTD: often <strong>normal or minimally abnormal EEG</strong> in early stages — useful differentiator from AD/DLB</li>
        <li>VaD: focal slow-wave activity corresponding to infarct locations</li>
        <li>NPH: diffuse theta slowing, improves post-shunt in responders</li>
        <li>Quantitative EEG (network connectivity) emerging as diagnostic adjunct</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Pijnenburg et al., J Neurol Neurosurg Psychiatry 2004</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SLIDE 9 — EVOKED POTENTIALS
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="slide slide-content" data-toc="Evoked Potentials">
  <h2>Evoked Potentials in Dementia</h2>
  <div class="info-box">
    <p>Evoked potentials (EPs) measure brain electrical responses to stimuli and reflect synaptic integrity of specific pathways. They are non-invasive, cost-effective, and sensitive to early neurodegeneration.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cards cards-3" style="margin-top:14px;">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>P300 (Cognitive ERP)</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Prolonged P300 latency</strong> — most consistent finding in AD (normal ~300–350 ms; AD: &gt;400 ms)</li>
        <li>Reduced P300 amplitude reflects attention/memory network dysfunction</li>
        <li>Positive correlation with severity of cognitive decline</li>
        <li>Meta-analysis: P300 latency prolonged in MCI → AD conversion (Polich 2007)</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Polich et al., Clin Neurophysiol 2007; Ally et al., Brain Cogn 2009</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>N200 &amp; MMN</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>N200 latency prolonged</strong> in AD; reflects pre-attentive auditory discrimination</li>
        <li>Mismatch negativity (MMN) amplitude reduced in moderate AD</li>
        <li>MMN relatively preserved in mild AD vs. schizophrenia</li>
        <li>MMN may detect processing deficits before clinical dementia</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Gaeta et al., Neuropsychologia 1999</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>VEP, BAEP, SSEP</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>VEP (P100)</strong>: prolonged in AD; reflects posterior cortical/visual association area dysfunction</li>
        <li><strong>BAEP</strong>: relatively preserved in AD, abnormal in CJD (brainstem involvement)</li>
        <li><strong>SSEP N20</strong>: generally normal in early AD; abnormal in CJD and vascular brainstem lesions</li>
        <li>Flash VEP amplitude correlates with occipital atrophy in DLB</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Filipovic et al., J Neurophysiol 1990; Babiloni et al., Clin Neurophysiol 2016</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:12px;">
    <strong>Clinical Pearl:</strong> In rapid-onset dementia, the combination of PSWCs on EEG + abnormal BAEP/SSEP + 14-3-3 protein in CSF strongly supports sporadic CJD (sensitivity ~90% combined, Zerr et al., <em>Brain</em> 2009).
  </div>
</div>

