<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SLIDE 12 — AD CLINICAL FEATURES
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="slide slide-content" data-toc="AD Clinical Features">
  <h2>Alzheimer's Disease — Clinical Profile <span class="badge badge-ad">AD</span></h2>
  <div class="cards cards-3">
    <div class="card card-clin">
      <h3>Core Cognitive Features</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Episodic memory impairment</strong> — earliest and most prominent; poor recall, intrusions, rapid forgetting</li>
        <li>Semantic memory loss in later stages</li>
        <li>Visuospatial deficits (posterior cortical atrophy variant)</li>
        <li>Executive dysfunction and language impairment emerge later</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">McKhann et al., Alzheimers Dement 2011</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-clin">
      <h3>Behavioural / Neuropsychiatric</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>Apathy (most common NPS, ~50–70%)</li>
        <li>Depression (30–40%), anxiety (20–30%)</li>
        <li>Agitation/aggression in moderate-to-severe stages</li>
        <li>Psychosis (delusions ~20–30%, hallucinations ~10–15%)</li>
        <li>Wandering; sleep disturbance (REM intact early)</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Lyketsos et al., JAMA 2002; Steinberg et al., Am J Psychiatry 2008</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-clin">
      <h3>Motor &amp; Other</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>Motor features absent in early–mild AD</li>
        <li>Extrapyramidal signs in late stages (parkinsonian features ~10%)</li>
        <li>Primitive reflexes (snout, grasp) emerge late</li>
        <li>Seizures: ~10–20% lifetime risk (especially young-onset, APOE ε4)</li>
        <li>Loss of ADLs: IADL before BADL (Lawton &amp; Brody hierarchy)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:14px;">
    <strong>Diagnostic Criteria (NIA-AA 2011, revised 2024):</strong> Probable AD dementia requires (1) insidious onset, (2) worsening cognitive history, (3) cognitive deficits on exam in memory + other domains, (4) no better explanation. Biomarkers (amyloid-A, tau-T, neurodegeneration-N) stratify certainty into high/intermediate/low probability.
    <span class="cite">McKhann et al., Alzheimers Dement 2011; Jack et al., Alzheimers Dement 2024</span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SLIDE 13 — VaD CLINICAL FEATURES
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="slide slide-content" data-toc="Vascular Dementia Clinical Features">
  <h2>Vascular Dementia — Clinical Profile <span class="badge badge-vasc">VaD</span></h2>
  <div class="cards cards-2">
    <div class="card card-clin">
      <h3>Core Features</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Executive dysfunction dominant</strong> — working memory, processing speed, attention affected &gt; episodic memory</li>
        <li>Stepwise or abrupt onset (post-stroke VaD) vs. gradual (small vessel disease)</li>
        <li>Focal neurological deficits consistent with stroke(s)</li>
        <li>Temporal relationship between vascular event and cognitive decline (within 3 months)</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Román et al., Neurology 1993; Sachdev et al., BMC Med 2014 (VASCOG criteria)</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-clin">
      <h3>Subtype-Specific Features</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Multi-infarct dementia</strong>: focal deficits, abrupt steps, Hachinski Ischemic Score ≥7</li>
        <li><strong>Subcortical VaD (SVD)</strong>: gait disorder ("marche à petits pas"), urinary urgency/incontinence, pseudobulbar affect, without focal signs</li>
        <li><strong>CADASIL</strong>: migraine with aura (onset), recurrent strokes, psychiatric features, dementia</li>
        <li><strong>Strategic infarct dementia</strong>: single infarct in thalamus, caudate, hippocampus → dense amnesia</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-clin">
      <h3>Behavioural &amp; Neuropsychiatric</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>Depression (most common NPS, ~40–50%)</li>
        <li>Apathy, emotional lability</li>
        <li>Pseudobulbar affect (frontal circuit damage)</li>
        <li>Less prominent hallucinations vs. DLB</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-clin">
      <h3>Motor Features</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>Gait disturbance: early, prominent in SVD</li>
        <li>Pyramidal signs (hyperreflexia, Babinski)</li>
        <li>Dysarthria/dysphagia in bilateral vascular disease</li>
        <li>Parkinsonism (lower body): shuffling gait without tremor</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SLIDE 14 — DLB CLINICAL FEATURES
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="slide slide-content" data-toc="DLB Clinical Features">
  <h2>Dementia with Lewy Bodies — Clinical Profile <span class="badge badge-dlb">DLB</span></h2>
  <div class="info-box">
    <strong>Core Clinical Features (≥2 for probable DLB; ≥1 with indicative biomarker):</strong>
    <ul class="check" style="margin-top:8px;">
      <li>Fluctuating cognition with pronounced variation in attention and alertness</li>
      <li>Recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations (typically people, animals)</li>
      <li>REM sleep behaviour disorder (RBD) — may precede dementia by decades</li>
      <li>One or more spontaneous cardinal features of parkinsonism</li>
    </ul>
    <span class="cite">McKeith et al., Neurology 2017 (Fourth Consensus Report DLB Consortium)</span>
  </div>
  <div class="cards cards-3" style="margin-top:14px;">
    <div class="card card-clin">
      <h3>Cognitive Profile</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>Attentional and executive dysfunction dominant</li>
        <li>Visuospatial deficits prominent (pentagons, clock drawing)</li>
        <li>Memory relatively preserved early (unlike AD)</li>
        <li>Fluctuation: delirium-like episodes, daytime somnolence</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-clin">
      <h3>Autonomic Features</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>Orthostatic hypotension (↑ falls risk)</li>
        <li>Constipation, urinary symptoms</li>
        <li>Reduced cardiac MIBG uptake (biomarker)</li>
        <li>Excessive daytime sleepiness</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-clin">
      <h3>Key Differentiator from AD</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>Pronounced neuroleptic sensitivity (contraindicated)</li>
        <li>Earlier parkinsonism and hallucinations</li>
        <li>Preserved medial temporal volume on MRI</li>
        <li>Abnormal DaTSCAN distinguishes from AD/VaD (Sp &gt;85%)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SLIDE 15 — FTD CLINICAL FEATURES
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="slide slide-content" data-toc="FTD Clinical Features">
  <h2>Frontotemporal Dementia — Clinical Profile <span class="badge badge-ftd">FTD</span></h2>
  <div class="cards cards-2">
    <div class="card card-clin">
      <h3>bvFTD — Behavioural Variant</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Early, prominent behavioural/personality change</strong> (disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy)</li>
        <li>Perseverative/compulsive behaviour; dietary changes (carbohydrate craving, hyperorality)</li>
        <li>Executive dysfunction &gt; memory in early stages</li>
        <li>Early loss of social cognition (theory of mind)</li>
        <li>Average onset age 58 (younger than AD); genetic forms: C9ORF72, MAPT, GRN</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Rascovsky et al., Brain 2011 (International Behavioural Variant FTD Criteria Consortium)</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-clin">
      <h3>PPA Variants (Primary Progressive Aphasia)</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Nonfluent/Agrammatic (PNFA)</strong>: effortful, halting speech; agrammatism; apraxia of speech; MAPT/GRN pathology</li>
        <li><strong>Semantic (SD)</strong>: fluent but empty speech; anomia; loss of word/object meaning; TDP-43 type C</li>
        <li><strong>Logopenic (lvPPA)</strong>: word-finding pauses, impaired sentence repetition; <em>usually AD pathology</em></li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Gorno-Tempini et al., Neurology 2011</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-clin">
      <h3>FTD-Motor Neuron Disease</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>bvFTD + ALS overlap in ~15% of FTD cases</li>
        <li>C9ORF72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion (most common genetic cause of FTD-ALS)</li>
        <li>Rapid progression; poor prognosis (2–3 yr median survival after MND onset)</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Dejesus-Hernandez et al., Neuron 2011</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-clin">
      <h3>PSP &amp; CBD (FTD Spectrum)</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>PSP (Richardson syndrome)</strong>: vertical supranuclear gaze palsy, axial rigidity, falls, frontal dementia</li>
        <li><strong>CBD syndrome</strong>: limb apraxia, asymmetric parkinsonism, alien limb, cortical sensory loss, myoclonus</li>
        <li>Both are 4R tauopathies (MAPT mutations in familial forms)</li>
        <li>"Hummingbird/penguin" midbrain sign on sagittal MRI (PSP)</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Armstrong et al., Neurology 2013 (PSP); Armstrong et al., Neurology 2013 (CBD criteria)</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

