<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SLIDE 17 — RARE DEMENTIAS CLINICAL FEATURES
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="slide slide-content" data-toc="Rare Dementias">
  <h2>Rapidly Progressive &amp; Rare Dementias</h2>
  <div class="cards cards-3">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD)</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>Rapid progression (weeks–months) to severe dementia + death</li>
        <li><strong>Triad</strong>: rapidly progressive dementia, myoclonus, ataxia</li>
        <li>Periodic sharp wave complexes on EEG (sCJD)</li>
        <li>DWI cortical ribboning + basal ganglia/thalamic signal (pulvinar sign in vCJD)</li>
        <li>CSF RT-QuIC &gt;95% specificity (Foutz et al., Ann Neurol 2017)</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Zerr et al., Brain 2009; WHO diagnostic criteria</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus (NPH)</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li><strong>Hakim's triad</strong>: gait apraxia → incontinence → dementia (in temporal order)</li>
        <li>Gait: wide-based, magnetic, shuffling; frontally patterned</li>
        <li>Dementia: subcortical pattern; executive &gt; memory</li>
        <li>Evans index &gt;0.3 on CT; DESH pattern on MRI</li>
        <li>Tap test (+CSF drainage) predicts shunt response</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Relkin et al., Neurosurgery 2005</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Autoimmune Encephalitis / Paraneoplastic</h3>
      <ul class="bullet">
        <li>Subacute onset; psychiatric features dominant early (psychosis, agitation)</li>
        <li>NMDA-R encephalitis: psychiatric → seizures → movement disorder → coma</li>
        <li>LGI1 encephalitis: faciobrachial dystonic seizures (pathognomonic), hyponatraemia</li>
        <li>VGKC complex, AMPA-R, GABA-B: various phenotypes</li>
        <li>Treatment-responsive — early recognition critical</li>
      </ul>
      <span class="cite">Dalmau et al., Lancet Neurol 2011; Irani et al., Brain 2010</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="warn-box" style="margin-top:14px;">
    <strong>Reversible dementia causes to exclude:</strong> Hypothyroidism, B12/folate deficiency, neurosyphilis, HIV, NPH, chronic SDH, medication toxicity. A systematic workup (bloods, LP, neuroimaging) must precede dementia diagnosis.
    <span class="cite">NICE NG97 2018; AAN Practice Parameter 2001</span>
  </div>
</div>

